  "properties": {
    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "##Text Field Search\r\n\r\nYour goal is to finish and test a text-field search that helps students quickly find study resources. The function should:\r\n\r\n- Take `data`, `field`, and `query`.\r\n- Trim extra spaces from the query.\r\n- Compare strings in a case-insensitive way.\r\n- Show **all resources** when the query is empty.\r\n- Skip resources that are missing the target field.\r\n- Not change the original dataset.\r\n\r\n::: details [Steps]\r\n\r\n1. In `script.js`, find the `searchByField(data, field, query)` function.\r\n2. Use the AI helper (CHAT Co-Build):\r\n   - Paste just this function into the chat.\r\n   - Use the C–H–A–T structure:\r\n     - **Clarify**: Ask AI to restate what the function should do with `data`, `field`, and `query`, and how it affects what shows in the results area (the list of study resources and match counts).\r\n     - **Hone**: Ask for help adding:\r\n       - `trim()` to clean the query.\r\n       - Lowercasing on both sides before comparing.\r\n       - A guard so resources missing `item[field]` are skipped safely.\r\n3. **Apply** Implement or refine the function based on the AI’s suggestions:\r\n   - Build a `results` array.\r\n   - Apply the trimmed, lowercased query.\r\n   - If the trimmed query is empty, **return all resources** (so students can browse everything).\r\n   \r\n5. Test Cases for the Study Hub:\r\n   - **Mixed case**: search for a resource title or subject using different capitalization (e.g., `\"QUADRATICS\"`, `\"quadratics\"`, `\"Quadratics\"`).\r\n   - **Leading/trailing spaces**: search with extra spaces around a term (e.g., `\"  plant cells  \"`).\r\n   - **Empty query**: clear the input and run search; all study resources should appear again.\r\n   - **Unknown term**: use a query that matches nothing (e.g., `\"unicorn derivatives\"`); should show “0” matches (optional: show a “No resources found” message).\r\n6. In the backpack, save a reusable prompt **Template** you might use in the future, with placeholders like `{data}`, `{field}`, `{query}`, `{targetId}` that are specific to a study resource search (e.g., `{subject}`, `{resourceType}`, `{tags}`).\r\n:::",
